1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,542
This is the revision for module one, Statistical and Data Foundations for EDA.

2
00:00:05,542 --> 00:00:07,176
The module takeaway is:

3
00:00:07,176 --> 00:00:11,084
understand data structure and distributional behaviour.

4
00:00:11,084 --> 00:00:16,200
We recap lectures one to six and finish with questions to test yourself.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:18,521
Lectures one and two.

6
00:00:18,521 --> 00:00:22,505
E D A is a habit that runs through the whole lifecycle.

7
00:00:22,505 --> 00:00:26,852
Explore freely and confirm strictly, never on the same rows.

8
00:00:26,852 --> 00:00:31,850
A bigger sample shrinks noise, but only a better sample removes bias.

9
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:34,168
Plot first and summarise second:

10
00:00:34,168 --> 00:00:44,816
skewness shows which side the tail is on, kurtosis shows how heavy the tails are, and the one point five I Q R fence flags outliers to investigate.

11
00:00:45,616 --> 00:00:47,383
Lectures three and four.

12
00:00:47,383 --> 00:00:56,363
The four scales of measurement are nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio, and the scale decides which statistics are valid.

13
00:00:56,363 --> 00:01:00,559
Discrete data is counted and continuous data is measured.

14
00:01:00,559 --> 00:01:06,006
Structured data fits a table, semi-structured data uses tags like J S O N.

15
00:01:06,006 --> 00:01:09,907
Text is broken into corpus, document, token and term.

16
00:01:09,907 --> 00:01:13,072
And time series have trend and seasonality.

17
00:01:13,872 --> 00:01:15,351
Lectures five and six.

18
00:01:15,351 --> 00:01:23,215
An image is a matrix of brightness values and audio is a list of amplitudes, so everything becomes a grid of numbers.

19
00:01:23,215 --> 00:01:31,280
The curse of dimensionality says every extra feature multiplies the space, so data thins out and distances lose meaning.

20
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:39,480
Bias enters at collection and labelling, long before the model, so split by group and write down what the data cannot say.

21
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:42,059
The labs for this module:

22
00:01:42,059 --> 00:01:55,007
practicals one and two frame a problem, load data, and compare mean with median and variance with I Q R; the California housing example does a full univariate E D A and pivot tables.

23
00:01:55,007 --> 00:01:57,995
Practicals three and four spot data types.

24
00:01:57,995 --> 00:02:02,192
Practicals five and six turn images and audio into numbers.

25
00:02:02,992 --> 00:02:04,736
Test yourself, part one.

26
00:02:04,736 --> 00:02:05,681
Question one:

27
00:02:05,681 --> 00:02:09,532
a column has mean ninety five and median thirty nine.

28
00:02:09,532 --> 00:02:13,310
What does that tell you, and which would you report?

29
00:02:13,310 --> 00:02:13,819
Answer:

30
00:02:13,819 --> 00:02:19,414
the data is right-skewed by an extreme value, so report the median and I Q R.

31
00:02:19,414 --> 00:02:20,359
Question two:

32
00:02:20,359 --> 00:02:25,082
why does collecting ten times more data not remove sampling bias?

33
00:02:25,082 --> 00:02:25,591
Answer:

34
00:02:25,591 --> 00:02:31,840
more rows shrink random error but bias stays, because the sampling design is the same.

35
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:34,581
Test yourself, part two.

36
00:02:34,581 --> 00:02:35,794
Question three:

37
00:02:35,794 --> 00:02:40,081
is temperature in Celsius interval or ratio, and why?

38
00:02:40,081 --> 00:02:40,647
Answer:

39
00:02:40,647 --> 00:02:44,933
interval, because zero degrees is not no temperature.

40
00:02:44,933 --> 00:02:46,066
Question four:

41
00:02:46,066 --> 00:02:50,271
why must you scale features before P C A or K-Means?

42
00:02:50,271 --> 00:02:50,837
Answer:

43
00:02:50,837 --> 00:02:56,337
otherwise the largest-unit feature dominates distances and variance.

44
00:02:56,337 --> 00:02:57,469
Question five:

45
00:02:57,469 --> 00:03:02,403
name two reasons a point may sit far outside the I Q R fence.

46
00:03:02,403 --> 00:03:02,969
Answer:

47
00:03:02,969 --> 00:03:08,064
a data error, a rare but real value, or a different population.

48
00:03:08,864 --> 00:03:10,452
That is module one.

49
00:03:10,452 --> 00:03:11,205
Remember:

50
00:03:11,205 --> 00:03:23,242
plot first, summarise second; median and I Q R when data is skewed; the scale decides the statistic; and bias is fixed at design, not by volume.

51
00:03:23,242 --> 00:03:26,920
Next, module two covers core advanced E D A.

52
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:28,592
Thanks for watching.

